      <h1>Screenshots in Git</h1>
      <p class=3D"standfirst">
        Every pasted screenshot becomes a permanent blob in the data repo. =
Here are the
        alternatives for storing images and their history space-efficiently=
 =E2=80=94 without
        giving up the property that a cloned spec repo carries its own pict=
ures.
      </p>
      <div class=3D"masthead-rule"></div>
    </header>

    <div class=3D"verdict-box">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Normalize images to WebP in the browser before upload. Roughly =
an order of magnitude, contained to two files, no git-engine changes.</li>
        <li>Hash the <em>source</em> bytes, not the encoded output, or dedu=
plication quietly breaks across machines.</li>
        <li>Pick a lineage model only if you want a versions UI. If <code>g=
it log</code> answering the question is enough, mutable names are far simpl=
er.</li>
        <li>Skip Git LFS and external object stores. Both break something t=
he product currently promises.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id=3D"problems">
      <div class=3D"section-head">
        <p class=3D"kicker">Framing</p>
        <h2>Two problems wearing one coat</h2>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"col">
        <p>
          "Space-efficient storage of images and image history" reads as on=
e question but
          decomposes into two, and the good answers barely overlap.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"split">
        <article>
          <h3>Bytes</h3>
          <p>
            The data repo grows monotonically. Every uploaded blob lives in=
 history forever,
            including images no spec references any more. Git delta-compres=
ses PNG and WebP
            essentially not at all =E2=80=94 they are already compressed =
=E2=80=94 so a 2&nbsp;MB screenshot
            is 2&nbsp;MB in every clone, permanently.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Lineage</h3>
          <p>
            There is none today. Content-addressed names mean re-shooting t=
he same login page
            yields <code>login-a1b2c3d4.png</code> and <code>login-9f8e7d6c=
.png</code> as
            unrelated files, and every spec pointing at the old one has to =
be edited by hand.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id=3D"constraints">
      <div class=3D"section-head">
        <p class=3D"kicker">What rules options out</p>
        <h2>Three constraints prune the space before you start</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class=3D"constraints">
        <li>
          <span class=3D"label">Three git engines</span>
          <p>
            <code>src/server/git/</code> ships the gh CLI, system git, and =
bundled
            <code>just-git</code> backends. Anything needing <code>git-lfs<=
/code> or
            clean/smudge filters breaks the bundled engine =E2=80=94 the on=
e that exists precisely
            so the app works on a machine with no git installed.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class=3D"label">Self-contained repo</span>
          <p>
            A stated product value: images live in the data repo like every=
thing else, and
            are browsable on GitHub under <code>.images/</code>. Clone the =
repo somewhere
            else and the pictures come with it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class=3D"label">Offline desktop use</span>
          <p>
            The Electron build renders specs without a network. Anything th=
at fetches image
            bytes on demand fails there, or needs an explicit local cache.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id=3D"bytes">
      <div class=3D"section-head">
        <p class=3D"kicker">Axis =E2=80=94 bytes per image</p>
        <h2>Shrink the picture before git ever sees it</h2>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"col">
        <p>
          This is the largest win available and the smallest change to make=
. A macOS retina
          clipboard screenshot arrives as a 1.5=E2=80=934&nbsp;MB PNG. Down=
scaled to about 1600&nbsp;px
          and encoded WebP at quality 80, the same image is 120=E2=80=93250=
&nbsp;KB and visually
          indistinguishable inside a scenario body.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class=3D"budget">
        <p class=3D"budget-title">Repo weight after 100 pasted screenshots<=
/p>
        <p class=3D"budget-sub">Estimate =C2=B7 typical retina capture =C2=
=B7 uncompressible in git either way</p>
        <div class=3D"bars">
          <div class=3D"bar-row">
            <div class=3D"bar-label">Today =E2=80=94 raw PNG <span>=C2=B7 ~=
2.4 MB each</span></div>
            <div class=3D"bar-track">
              <div class=3D"bar" style=3D"width: 82%;"></div>
              <div class=3D"bar-value">240 MB</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class=3D"bar-row">
            <div class=3D"bar-label">Normalized =E2=80=94 WebP q80 <span>=
=C2=B7 ~180 KB each</span></div>
            <div class=3D"bar-track">
              <div class=3D"bar is-after" style=3D"width: 6.2%;"></div>
              <div class=3D"bar-value">18 MB</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class=3D"budget-foot">
          About 13=C3=97 smaller, in the working tree <em>and</em> in histo=
ry forever. The gap widens
          with every future upload, which is why this lever outranks everyt=
hing below it.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <div class=3D"options">
        <div class=3D"option">
          <div class=3D"option-head">
            <h3>Normalize client-side, in the editor</h3>
            <span class=3D"chip chip-adopt">Adopt</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            In <code>MarkdownEditor.tsx</code>, before the POST:
            <code>createImageBitmap</code> =E2=86=92 <code>OffscreenCanvas<=
/code> =E2=86=92
            <code>convertToBlob({type:"image/webp", quality:0.8})</code>. Z=
ero server
            dependencies, which matters =E2=80=94 Bun has no built-in encod=
er, and <code>sharp</code>
            is a native module that would complicate the Electron build. Pa=
ss SVG through
            untouched, skip the transcode under about 200&nbsp;KB, and feat=
ure-detect WebP
            encoding with a fall back to the original bytes.
          </p>
          <div class=3D"ledger">
            <div>
              <span class=3D"label">Gain</span>
              <p>~13=C3=97, on every image, forever. Two files touched. Wor=
ks identically on all three git engines. EXIF is stripped, which is a quiet=
 privacy win.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span class=3D"label">Cost</span>
              <p>Lossy. Diagrams with small text can suffer, so offer a "ke=
ep original" escape hatch on the upload path.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class=3D"callout">
        <p class=3D"kicker">Design around this</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Hash the source bytes, not the encoded output.</strong> T=
wo machines running
          different browser encoder versions produce different WebP bytes f=
rom the same
          screenshot =E2=80=94 so hashing the output silently breaks the "p=
aste the same screenshot
          twice, reuse one file" guarantee the spec makes. Name it
          <code>&lt;slug&gt;-&lt;hash of original&gt;.webp</code> and dedup=
lication stays exact.
          Immutable caching still holds, because <code>storeImage</code> ne=
ver rewrites a name
          that already exists.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id=3D"history">
      <div class=3D"section-head">
        <p class=3D"kicker">Axis =E2=80=94 keeping bytes out of history</p>
        <h2>Five ways to stop paying for old blobs</h2>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"col">
        <p>
          If normalization is not enough =E2=80=94 a repo with thousands of=
 images, or one already
          heavy =E2=80=94 these are the structural options. Only one of the=
m is cheap.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class=3D"table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope=3D"col">Option</th>
              <th scope=3D"col">Verdict</th>
              <th scope=3D"col">Reasoning</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Git LFS</td>
              <td class=3D"verdict"><span class=3D"chip chip-reject">Reject=
</span></td>
              <td><p>Breaks the bundled engine, adds a GitHub quota and bil=
ling dependency, and turns files into pointer text in the GitHub UI =E2=80=
=94 losing the browsability the spec explicitly calls out.</p></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Partial clone</td>
              <td class=3D"verdict"><span class=3D"chip chip-consider">Cons=
ider</span></td>
              <td><p>One flag in <code>clone()</code>: <code>--filter=3Dblo=
b:limit=3D1m</code>. All spec text arrives, oversized image blobs are fetch=
ed lazily. Shrinks <em>local</em> clones only. Needs network on first rende=
r of an old image, so gate it off for desktop. Verify <code>just-git</code>=
 supports filters first.</p></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Orphan branch</td>
              <td class=3D"verdict"><span class=3D"chip chip-reject">Reject=
</span></td>
              <td><p>Keeps images in one repo but out of the default workin=
g tree. Needs manual object plumbing across three engines and loses side-by=
-side browsing. Poor complexity-to-payoff ratio.</p></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Separate images repo</td>
              <td class=3D"verdict"><span class=3D"chip chip-consider">Cons=
ider</span></td>
              <td><p>Bounds the spec repo permanently. Costs two auth and s=
ync paths, and forfeits atomicity =E2=80=94 a spec commit and its image com=
mit can now diverge.</p></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>External store</td>
              <td class=3D"verdict"><span class=3D"chip chip-reject">Reject=
</span></td>
              <td><p>Smallest repo, but forfeits self-containment outright:=
 a repo cloned elsewhere has dead image links. Defensible only as an option=
al mirror layered over repo-resident blobs.</p></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id=3D"reclaim">
      <div class=3D"section-head">
        <p class=3D"kicker">Axis =E2=80=94 reclaiming what is already there=
</p>
        <h2>Orphans are cheap to collect; history is not</h2>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"col">
        <p>
          An <code>images gc</code> maintenance command: scan every <code>.=
feature.md</code>
          at HEAD for <code>![](/api/images/=E2=80=A6)</code> references, t=
hen delete unreferenced
          files in <code>.images/</code> as an ordinary commit. That shrink=
s the checkout =E2=80=94
          not history.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reclaiming history needs <code>git filter-repo</code> or BFG plus=
 a force push,
          which invalidates every other clone of the repo. It belongs behin=
d an explicit,
          loudly confirmed operation, and never runs automatically.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id=3D"lineage">
      <div class=3D"section-head">
        <p class=3D"kicker">Axis =E2=80=94 image history</p>
        <h2>Three models for what "version 2 of this screenshot" means</h2>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"col">
        <p>
          Choose by what you want the product to <em>do</em>. Only one of t=
hese buys you a
          versions UI; the others just make the question answerable.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class=3D"options">
        <div class=3D"option">
          <div class=3D"option-head">
            <h3>Mutable names =E2=80=94 history comes free from git</h3>
            <span class=3D"chip chip-consider">Simplest</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Drop the hash. Store <code>.images/login-screen.webp</code> and=
 overwrite it on
            re-upload. Git history <em>becomes</em> the image history:
            <code>git log -- .images/login-screen.webp</code> gives every v=
ersion with author
            and date, at no cost. Updating a screenshot touches no spec fil=
e at all.
          </p>
          <div class=3D"ledger">
            <div>
              <span class=3D"label">Gain</span>
              <p>Real ordered history for free. Specs never churn when an i=
mage is refreshed.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span class=3D"label">Cost</span>
              <p>Loses free deduplication and the immutable cache header =
=E2=80=94 switch to ETag plus <code>must-revalidate</code>, or a <code>?v=
=3D&lt;hash&gt;</code> query.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class=3D"option">
          <div class=3D"option-head">
            <h3>Stable id over content-addressed blobs</h3>
            <span class=3D"chip chip-consider">Most capable</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Specs reference <code>/api/images/&lt;logical-id&gt;</code>; a =
manifest resolves
            the id to the current hash; every version persists as an immuta=
ble
            content-addressed blob. Preserves deduplication, preserves immu=
table caching, and
            yields an explicit ordered version list you can render as UI =
=E2=80=94 "3 versions,
            updated 2 weeks ago".
          </p>
          <div class=3D"ledger">
            <div>
              <span class=3D"label">Gain</span>
              <p>The only option that supports a genuine versions interface=
 without giving anything else up.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span class=3D"label">Cost</span>
              <p>A manifest file, which is a merge-conflict magnet under co=
ncurrent edits =E2=80=94 though <code>src/server/automerge/</code> may alre=
ady cover exactly this.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class=3D"option">
          <div class=3D"option-head">
            <h3>Sidecar metadata only</h3>
            <span class=3D"chip chip-reject">Weakest</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Keep today's scheme and add <code>.images/index.json</code> rec=
ording lineage.
            Cheapest bolt-on, but the manifest and the filesystem drift apa=
rt the first time
            anyone edits the repo outside the app =E2=80=94 which, for a gi=
t-backed product, is a
            question of when.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id=3D"recommend">
      <div class=3D"section-head">
        <p class=3D"kicker">Recommendation</p>
        <h2>Normalization first; decide lineage on product grounds</h2>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"col">
        <p>
          Ship client-side WebP normalization before anything else. It is c=
ontained to
          <code>MarkdownEditor.tsx</code> plus the hashing change in <code>=
storeImage</code>,
          requires no git-engine work, and cuts growth by an order of magni=
tude =E2=80=94 which likely
          makes the entire "keep bytes out of history" axis unnecessary for=
 years.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then pick a lineage model on a product question rather than a sto=
rage one: do you
          want a versions UI? If yes, stable ids over content-addressed blo=
bs. If
          <code>git log</code> answering the question is enough, mutable na=
mes get you there
          with far less machinery.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer>
      <span>Specdriven</span>
      <span>src/server/images.ts =C2=B7 src/frontend/MarkdownEditor.tsx</sp=
an>
      <span>Design options, not a decision</span>
    </footer>
  </main>
</div>

